    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the perimeter of a triangle, we simply add the lengths of all its sides. In this case, the sides are 14 cm, 8 cm, and 9 cm.\n\nPerimeter = 14 cm + 8 cm + 9 cm = 31 cm\n\nSo, the perimeter of the triangle is 31 cm.\n\nTo check my work, I can use the Pythagorean theorem to verify that the triangle is a right triangle. If it is, then the perimeter should be correct.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nLet's check if the triangle is a right triangle:\n\n14^2 = 9^2 + 8^2\n196 = 81 + 64\n196 = 145\n\nSince the equation is not true, the triangle is not a right triangle.\n\nHowever, even if the triangle is not a right triangle, the perimeter calculated using the lengths of the sides is still correct. The perimeter is simply the sum of the lengths of the sides, and the lengths of the sides are given as 14 cm, 8 cm, and 9 cm. Therefore, the perimeter of the triangle is 31 cm.",
